{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ at the point $(0,0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-36754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-36754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c00b5d9e6f1ffc7d1fdd4412ddec3f519190dcdb5bbf903fb0d900a1b45bd54"} {"question" "q-36754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-36754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-36754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve $2x+y-1=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c00b5d9e6f1ffc7d1fdd4412ddec3f519190dcdb5bbf903fb0d900a1b45bd54"} {"question" "q-71388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2y=1"] ["math" "x+y=1"] ["math" "x-2y=1"] ["math" "x-y=1"] ["math" "y-x=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-y=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4(\\frac{1}{2})-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c00b5d9e6f1ffc7d1fdd4412ddec3f519190dcdb5bbf903fb0d900a1b45bd54"} {"question" "q-71407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r=⟨-3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,-4,1⟩"] ["math" "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,4,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-71407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-130718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-130718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-130718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c00b5d9e6f1ffc7d1fdd4412ddec3f519190dcdb5bbf903fb0d900a1b45bd54"} {"question" "q-130718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-130718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+y-7=0"] ["math" "x+3y-6=0"] ["math" "x+y-4=0"] ["math" "x-3y=0"] ["math" "x-y-2=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-130718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+y-4=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-131115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-131115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-131115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c00b5d9e6f1ffc7d1fdd4412ddec3f519190dcdb5bbf903fb0d900a1b45bd54"} {"question" "q-131115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-131115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-j+k"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k"] ["math" "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k"] ["math" "i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"} {"question" "q-131115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad5d56ca32b5ccd8b20b3336e031f6e5cddcbc0a64410fee5bd7edcbe45060a5"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1785 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
